[{:seon.task/name "contracts-armed-at-definition"
  :seon.task/plan "Every definition happens once (AGENTS §5). `seon.contract/arm!` wraps an agent's REPL defn after it is defined, with alter-var-root, and `seon.contract/instrument!` arms the foundation namespaces at boot the same way. Correct: a definition carrying :malli/schema is defined already checked, from its definition event; arm!, instrument! and the ::wrapper metadata go. The contract-gap rule over a branch's changes is already one, `seon.merge/uncontracted`, which the writer's merge gate and root's judge both ask; the generated vars of defrecord/defprotocol are recognised from the definition events."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/a-contracted-definition-is-checked-from-its-definition
                      seon.contract-test/one-contract-gap-rule-agrees-everywhere}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-contract-is-checked-once-and-costs-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit's finding that exact :calls bounds make a new contract a cost regression (8c30ac5600 and 9c8b3d016c deleted contracts to save calls): \"Oh yeah contracts don't count against anything as long as you do it through the optimal interface and we aren't checking it multiple times for no reason. So understand what instrumentation does ensure we are instrumenting them and then if that is covering it then we don't need to check it multiple times if it's the same data.\" (docs/decisions.md). Four steps, in order. (1) What instrumentation does: how seon.contract/defn and seon.contract/wrap check args, returns and arity, where it is on (:seon.config.contract/instrument, the seven boot namespaces armed by seon.contract/instrument!, agents by fork), and what it misses: fns defined outside the defn macro, private fns, multimethods, protocol methods, anything excluded; written in the contract section of the wiki. (2) One interface: every contract is checked through that instrumentation; hand m/validate and m/explain calls and other ad hoc checks in src go through it or are deleted where the instrumented contract already covers them; each gap found in (1) is filled, so every contracted fn is instrumented. (3) No double checks: the same data validated more than once for no reason (a return checked and then the same value as the next fn's arg under the same schema; a hand check beside an instrumented one; a value re-checked each pass of a loop) is a class: find the cases, then design the mechanism that removes it (not case patches), and bring the design to the owner, through the context session, before building it. (4) Only then seon.cost stops counting the contract's own calls in :calls (seon.contract/wrap's counted wrapper); every pinned number moves once, approved under this ruling (the 616 and 8 included), and the merge report lists each old and new number. After it, a new contract on the one interface costs nothing on any proof, and a redundant re-check is still waste. Docs: docs/cost.md and the contract section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/every-contracted-fn-is-instrumented seon.cost-test/a-contracts-own-calls-are-not-counted}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-boundary-takes-keyed-schemas"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: contracts are armed everywhere, but what they say is loose: of about 1,780 :malli/schema in src about 125 answer :any and 151 answer :map, about 460 take a bare :map; :seon.error/base and entity-fact maps are open, so any extra key with any value passes. The change: a var property {:seon/boundary true}, starting with the edge functions of docs/failure-points.md, the store writers and the wire (server/answer, web/ui-call, model/sse-reply, model/request-body, repl/record!, my.root/load-example!); a boundary takes and answers named keyed schemas, no bare :map, :any or host object; bin/check contracts reports the count of loose returns and arguments with a ratchet that may only fall; maps the system writes are closed ({:closed true} on entity facts and error variants; [:map-of :keyword :seon.db/plain] where extension is wanted; agents' own schemas stay open); each tagged kind has one checked constructor (seon.render/as, the error constructor). The rule is written in AGENTS.md section 5 and docs/decisions.md, with the owner's words. The cost bounds are settled first by seon.contract/a-contract-is-checked-once-and-costs-nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.db/a-datom-its-schema-refuses-is-not-stored" "seon.render/a-render-tag-is-a-checked-schema" "seon.contract/a-contract-is-checked-once-and-costs-nothing"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.contract-test/a-loose-boundary-is-counted}}]
